                <pb facs="0624" n="526" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg526"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/2757: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-04-06T22:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 6,
                                1920—10 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 6—6:12
                    p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>559. Your 329, March 30, 5 p.m. and Embassy’s 545, April 1, 7 p.m. Formal reply
                    just received from Foreign Office to effect that British Chargé d’Affaires Tokyo
                    has been telegraphically informed that British Embassy [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Government?</hi>], while feeling that all reasonable consideration has
                    already been shown for views of the Japanese Government, are willing to concur
                    in exchange of letters suggested by Lamont provided that French and American
                    Governments approve likewise of the proposal. Paris informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
